Define a color as a record containing three unsigned bytes named red, green, and blue. Define a picture pixel as a record containing three unsigned bytes named red, green, and blue, followed by one unsigned byte named opacity. An opacity of zero means that the picture contributes none of its color. An opacity of 255 means that the picture completely replaces the color beneath it.

Define a frame as a width, a height, and the address of a row-major buffer of colors. The frame owns that buffer from the time the buffer is prepared until the frame releases it. Define a picture as a width, a height, and the address of a row-major buffer of picture pixels. The picture owns that buffer from the time the buffer is prepared until the picture releases it. Define a byte buffer as a capacity and the address of that many bytes. The byte buffer owns those bytes until it releases them.

Define prepare frame as a function that accepts the address of a frame named receiving frame, an integer named width, and an integer named height, and returns a truth value. Reject a missing receiving frame, a nonpositive dimension, or a multiplication that cannot fit in the machine's size type. Ask for one zeroed color for every pixel. If that request fails, return false without changing the receiving frame. Otherwise, place the width, height, and new buffer address in the receiving frame, give ownership of the new buffer to the receiving frame, and return true. This ends the definition of prepare frame.

Define release frame as a function that accepts the address of a frame named receiving frame. If the address is missing, do nothing. Otherwise, release the buffer owned by the receiving frame and replace its width, height, and buffer address with zero, zero, and no address. This ends the definition of release frame.

Define prepare picture and release picture in the same way, except that they allocate and release picture pixels. Define prepare byte buffer and release byte buffer in the same way, except that they allocate and release individual bytes.

Define pixel in frame as a function that accepts a frame, a column, and a row, and returns the address of the color at that column and row. The caller must supply coordinates inside the frame. Multiply the row by the frame's width, add the column, and use the sum as the position in the frame's buffer. This ends the definition of pixel in frame.

Define pixel in picture in the same way, except that it returns the address of a picture pixel in a picture's buffer.

Define paint frame one color as a function that accepts the address of a frame named receiving frame and a color. For each row starting at zero and stopping before the frame's height, visit each column starting at zero and stopping before the frame's width, and place the given color in the receiving pixel. This ends the definition of paint frame one color.

Define paint left picture as a function that accepts the address of a picture named receiving picture and an integer named frame number. The picture is a complete opaque picture, not a borrowed view of the final frame. For every receiving pixel, compute a purple and orange sky above three fifths of the picture and moving blue water below it. Compute a small yellow sun whose column follows a triangle wave as the frame number advances. Place the computed red, green, and blue bytes in the receiving pixel and set its opacity to 255. This ends the definition of paint left picture.

Define paint right picture as a function that accepts the address of a picture named receiving picture and an integer named frame number. For every receiving pixel, compute which eighteen-pixel checker cell receives it. Brighten pixels on the grid lines. Brighten a five-pixel vertical scanning line whose column advances twice for each new frame. Place the resulting purple colors in the receiving pixel and set its opacity to 255. This ends the definition of paint right picture.

Define paint marker picture as a function that accepts the address of a square picture named receiving picture and an integer named frame number. Regard the middle of the picture as the center of a circle. For every receiving pixel outside the circle, set opacity to zero. For every receiving pixel in the outer three-pixel ring, use a pale color and an opacity of 238. For every other receiving pixel inside the circle, compute a changing pink color from its displacement from the center and the frame number, and use an opacity of 168. This makes the marker a separately owned translucent picture. This ends the definition of paint marker picture.

Define blend channel as a function that accepts a foreground byte, a background byte, and an opacity byte, and returns one byte. Multiply the foreground by the opacity. Multiply the background by 255 minus the opacity. Add those products and 127, divide the result by 255, and return the quotient. This ends the definition of blend channel.

Define place picture over frame as a function that accepts the address of a frame named receiving frame, a read-only picture named source picture, an integer named receiving column, and an integer named receiving row. This function borrows both buffers and takes ownership of neither.

For each source row starting at zero and stopping before the source picture's height, add the receiving row to find its destination row. Skip that source row when its destination row lies outside the receiving frame. For each source column starting at zero and stopping before the source picture's width, add the receiving column to find its destination column. Skip that source pixel when its destination column lies outside the receiving frame. Read the source pixel and receive the destination pixel. Blend the source red over the destination red, the source green over the destination green, and the source blue over the destination blue, using the source pixel's opacity for all three channels. Place those three answers in the destination pixel. This ends both loops and the definition of place picture over frame.

Define stitch pictures into frame as a function that accepts the address of a frame named receiving frame, a read-only picture named left picture, and a read-only picture named right picture. Place the left picture over the receiving frame at column zero and row zero. Place the right picture over the receiving frame at the column immediately after the left picture and at row zero. This ends the definition of stitch pictures into frame.

Define triangle wave as a function that accepts an integer named step and a positive integer named maximum and returns an integer. Its period is twice the maximum. Reduce the step to a position in that period. When the position is beyond the maximum, return the period minus the position. Otherwise, return the position. This ends the definition of triangle wave.

Define write frame as portable pixmap as a function that accepts a read-only frame named source frame and the address of a byte buffer named receiving row buffer, and returns a truth value. It borrows both buffers and takes ownership of neither. Require three bytes of row-buffer capacity for every column. Write a P6 portable pixmap header containing the frame's width, height, and a maximum channel value of 255. For every row, copy each source pixel's red, green, and blue bytes in that order into the receiving row buffer, then write exactly that row buffer to standard output. Return false after any incomplete write. Return true after every row has been written. This ends the definition of write frame as portable pixmap.

Define the program as follows. Accept at most one argument. When present, the argument is the number of frames to write and must be an integer from one through ten thousand. When absent, write 120 frames.

Prepare one 320 by 180 frame, one 160 by 180 left picture, one 160 by 180 right picture, one 64 by 64 marker picture, and one 960-byte output row buffer. The program owns all five buffers. If any preparation fails, release every buffer that was successfully prepared and report failure.

For each frame number starting at zero and stopping before the requested frame count, paint the left picture, paint the right picture, and paint the marker picture. Paint the frame dark blue. Stitch the two opaque pictures into the frame. Compute a marker column that follows a triangle wave from just outside the left edge through the frame and back. Compute a marker row that follows a shorter triangle wave. Place the translucent marker picture over the stitched frame. Write the completed frame as one portable pixmap image on standard output. This ends the loop over frames.

Flush standard output. Release the output row buffer, marker picture, right picture, left picture, and frame, in that order. Report success unless writing failed. This ends the program.

The resulting standard-output stream is a sequence of self-contained RGB pictures. A video encoder can receive that stream directly. For example, at thirty frames per second, the ordinary shell command is: ./render-video | ffmpeg -f image2pipe -framerate 30 -i - -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p stitched.mp4
